(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-venous-disorders-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Venous Disorders Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 59-year-old man is about to start hemodialysis through a newly created forearm arteriovenous fistula. Which self-check should he be taught to confirm that the fistula is working?","choices":["Confirm the skin over it is cool","Confirm no sound is heard over it","Feel for a vibrating thrill","Confirm it stays full when raised"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A healthy arteriovenous (AV) fistula has a diffuse thrill and a soft bruit, both with systolic and diastolic components, so feeling the vibrating thrill over his forearm access is a simple check that it is working. Upper-extremity fistulas like his are created for long-term hemodialysis access. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman on hemodialysis with a large upper arm arteriovenous fistula has developed exertional dyspnea, orthopnea and bilateral leg edema over several weeks. The fistula has a strong thrill (a vibration felt over the access). Which complication of the fistula should be suspected?","choices":["Lymphedema of the arm","Heart failure","Superficial thrombophlebitis","Deep vein thrombosis of the leg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Exertional dyspnea, orthopnea and bilateral leg edema developing over weeks in a patient with a large arteriovenous (AV) fistula point to heart failure. Heart failure is one of the complications to watch for with a fistula, along with infection, chronic venous insufficiency and ischemia. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has long-standing swelling of both legs just above the ankles that is symmetric, with normal skin color and no ulcers or varicose veins. Cardiac, kidney and liver evaluations are normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lymphedema","Heart failure edema","Lipedema","Chronic venous insufficiency"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral, symmetric swelling just above the ankles in a woman is how lipedema is described. Her normal cardiac, kidney and liver evaluations exclude the systemic causes of bilateral edema, and the absence of varicose veins, ulcers or skin color change argues against venous disease. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman eight weeks postpartum has mild calf aching and a low pretest probability of deep vein thrombosis. Her clinician orders a D-dimer. Which characteristic makes a negative result useful for excluding a clot?","choices":["High specificity","Direct visualization of clot","Measurement of vein compressibility","High sensitivity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With a low pretest probability she starts with a D-dimer, and a negative result lets the clinician stop because the test is sensitive. Sensitivity is what makes it a rule-out test for deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism; it is not specific, so a positive result would still need ultrasound. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man is about to undergo treatment of symptomatic varicose veins and asks about the long-term outlook. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Recurrence is rare after treatment","Only appearance improves, not symptoms","One treatment is permanently curative","Varicose veins often recur"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Varicose veins often recur, and the goal of venous intervention is improvement in symptoms and appearance rather than a permanent cure. He should expect that the veins may return after treatment. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with superficial thrombophlebitis of the calf was started on compresses, elevation and an anti-inflammatory drug. At his recheck nine days later, the tender cord has lengthened and the redness has spread up the leg. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue the same treatment","Place an inferior vena cava filter","Reexamine again in four weeks","Repeat duplex ultrasound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. He had the standard recheck within 7 to 10 days, and the cord has lengthened and the redness spread, so his signs are worsening. Persistence or worsening at that recheck calls for a repeat duplex ultrasound; if it shows extensive clot burden, anticoagulation may be started. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man receiving chemotherapy for colon cancer comes to the emergency department with three days of left calf swelling that measures 4 cm more than the right, plus tenderness along the deep veins of the calf. He has no history of clots and nothing else explains his leg. The team debates whether to order a D-dimer or go straight to imaging. Using the Wells criteria, which is the best next step?","choices":["D-dimer testing first","Contrast ascending venography","Ankle-brachial index","Venous duplex ultrasound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Active cancer under treatment 1 + calf swelling 4 cm more than the other leg (over 3 cm) 1 + tenderness along the deep veins 1 = 3, a high likelihood of deep vein thrombosis (DVT). In the emergency department, where the score applies, a high-risk patient goes to ultrasound whether or not a D-dimer is drawn. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman seen in urgent care for mild left calf aching has a low Wells score. Her D-dimer is negative. What is the next step?","choices":["No further testing needed","Venous duplex ultrasound of the thigh","Contrast ascending venography","Repeat D-dimer in one week"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a low-likelihood patient, a negative D-dimer ends the work-up, so no further testing is needed. D-dimer is sensitive, which makes it a useful rule-out test for deep vein thrombosis (DVT); only a positive result would send her to ultrasound. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has a chronic leg ulcer whose appearance fits none of the venous, arterial or diabetic patterns. Examination and duplex ultrasound do not clarify the cause, and she has no signs of infection. Which is the best next step?","choices":["Superficial vein ablation","Catheter-based venography","Punch biopsy","Empiric oral antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When the cause of a leg ulcer is uncertain, a punch biopsy can help rule out other causes. History and examination cannot reliably exclude other causes of a leg ulcer, and the differential includes autoimmune disease, sickle cell anemia and erythema induratum. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with chronic venous insufficiency has two days of warmth, tenderness and spreading blanching redness of the lower leg, with a low-grade fever. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Oral flavonoid therapy","Oral cephalexin","Topical antibiotic ointment","Mid-potency topical corticosteroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two days of warmth, tenderness and spreading blanching redness with a low-grade fever in a leg with chronic venous insufficiency describe cellulitis. Oral antibiotics are recommended for this cellulitis, and cephalexin or clindamycin are the named choices. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has a first proximal deep vein thrombosis with no surgery, trauma, hormone use, travel, cancer or other identifiable trigger, and no inherited disorder has been found. How long should anticoagulation continue?","choices":["Three to six months","Six weeks","Indefinitely","Three months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His first proximal deep vein thrombosis had no surgery, trauma, hormones, travel, cancer or other trigger, which makes it an unprovoked first venous thromboembolism (VTE). An unprovoked first VTE is treated for an indefinite length of time, beyond the 3-month minimum that applies to every first episode. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has chronic leg swelling, brown discoloration from hemosiderin and a shallow ulcer above the medial ankle. Her pedal pulses are weak and her ankle-brachial index is 0.6 on that leg. Which treatment should be avoided until her arterial disease is addressed?","choices":["Skin emollients","Graduated compression","Gentle skin cleansing","Daily walking with ankle flexion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Compression therapy is the standard treatment for venous insufficiency, but peripheral artery disease must be ruled out before it is initiated. Her weak pedal pulses and ankle-brachial index of 0.6 point to arterial disease, so graduated compression waits until that is addressed. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 28, 31, 32"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a left forearm arteriovenous fistula created three months ago for hemodialysis. To check that it is healthy, her clinician raises the arm above the level of her heart. Which response is expected from a healthy fistula?","choices":["Its bruit turns systolic only","It collapses completely","It stays distended and tense","Her heart rate slows"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A healthy arteriovenous (AV) fistula collapses completely when the arm is elevated. It is also generally characterized by a diffuse thrill and a soft bruit, both with systolic and diastolic components. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 83, 85"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man with a first calf deep vein thrombosis is hemodynamically stable, has low bleeding risk and normal kidney function, and has a supportive spouse who will bring him to every follow-up visit. Which is the best setting for treatment?","choices":["Intensive care unit monitoring","Observation without treatment","Outpatient anticoagulation","Hospital admission for heparin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Outpatient therapy is appropriate for patients who are hemodynamically stable, have low bleeding risk and no renal insufficiency, and have good support and reliable follow-up. He meets every criterion, and his clot is in the calf, not an iliofemoral clot that would require admission. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with large varicose veins in the right leg has a painful, red, indurated cord along the medial calf that developed over two days. He is afebrile and has no swelling of the leg. Which study confirms the diagnosis and is considered the gold standard?","choices":["Venous duplex ultrasound","D-dimer assay","Contrast ascending venography","Ankle-brachial index"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A painful, red, indurated cord along a superficial calf vein in a man with varicose veins suggests superficial thrombophlebitis. Venous duplex ultrasound is the gold standard that confirms it, showing a noncompressible superficial vein with clot and thickening of the vein wall. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with prior deep vein thrombosis has leg edema and brown pigmentation. Duplex ultrasound is performed to explain her symptoms. Which finding is expected?","choices":["Noncompressible calcified arteries","Narrowed superficial femoral artery","Dilated lymphatic channels","Incompetent venous valves with reflux"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Venous duplex ultrasound assesses venous patency (is there a deep vein thrombosis?) and valvular competence (is there reflux?). In chronic venous insufficiency after a prior clot, with edema and brown pigmentation, the expected finding is incompetent valves with reflux. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a large arteriovenous fistula of the thigh that was diagnosed on physical examination. Before repair, the vascular surgeon needs the exact location and size of the connection. Which study provides this information?","choices":["Punch biopsy of the skin","Computed tomography angiography","Ankle-brachial index","Plain radiograph of the thigh"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The fistula has already been diagnosed on physical examination, and computed tomography angiography (CTA) and angiography can confirm it and provide its location and size. That is the information the surgeon needs before an acquired fistula is removed or reduced in size. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has a tender, red, firm segment along a forearm vein after a peripheral intravenous catheter was removed. Venous duplex ultrasound shows a superficial vein with a thickened wall. Which additional finding would establish thrombophlebitis rather than phlebitis alone?","choices":["Clot within the vein lumen","Thickening of the vein wall","Reflux through incompetent valves","Fluid collection around the vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The ultrasound already shows wall thickening, which by itself means phlebitis, inflammation of the vein wall without thrombus. Clot within the vein together with that thickening establishes thrombophlebitis; duplex ultrasound, the gold standard, shows it as a noncompressible superficial vein with clot. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with heart failure is admitted with a tender, swollen left calf, and duplex ultrasound confirms deep vein thrombosis. Her daughter asks why heart failure raises the risk of a clot. Which Virchow triad component should the clinician name?","choices":["Elevated platelet count","Venous stasis","Hypercoagulable state","Endothelial trauma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure is listed under venous stasis, the Virchow triad component of altered blood flow, alongside immobilization and venous insufficiency. Her heart failure raises clot risk through stasis; it is neither an injury to the vein wall nor an inherited or acquired clotting tendency. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with newly diagnosed deep vein thrombosis has chronic kidney disease with a creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min. Which approach should be avoided?","choices":["Overlapping heparin with warfarin","Beginning therapy as an outpatient","Admission for intravenous heparin","Adjusting heparin by lab results"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With a creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min, she needs to be hospitalized to start treatment, so beginning therapy at home is the approach to avoid. In hospital she receives unfractionated heparin (UFH) as an intravenous bolus followed by a continuous infusion, adjusted by laboratory results and overlapped with warfarin. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with a first deep vein thrombosis is otherwise healthy and hemodynamically stable. The plan is oral anticoagulation monotherapy with a loading phase followed by maintenance, without any injection lead-in. Which drug fits this strategy?","choices":["Dabigatran","Edoxaban","Fondaparinux","Apixaban"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral monotherapy with a loading dose followed by a maintenance dose, with no injection lead-in, is the strategy that uses rivaroxaban or apixaban, both anti-Xa agents. Apixaban (Eliquis) is a direct factor Xa inhibitor, and he is stable and otherwise healthy, so the oral-only plan fits. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman had femoral vessel puncture for an endovascular procedure two days ago. Examination of the groin shows a new bruit over the puncture site, and the surgeon suspects an iatrogenic arteriovenous fistula. Which study is used to confirm the diagnosis?","choices":["D-dimer assay","Ankle-brachial index","Plain radiograph of the groin","Duplex ultrasound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Puncture of the femoral vessels for an endovascular procedure is the classic setting for an iatrogenic arteriovenous (AV) fistula, and duplex ultrasound confirms the diagnosis in suspected cases. Computed tomography angiography and angiography can also confirm a fistula and show its location and size. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman receiving chemotherapy through a central venous catheter has two days of right arm swelling and aching. The catheter injuring the vein wall is the endothelial trauma component of the Virchow triad. Which is the best diagnostic test?","choices":["Venous duplex ultrasound","Contrast ascending venography","Ankle-brachial index","D-dimer alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two days of arm swelling and aching with a central venous catheter call for imaging of that limb, and venous duplex ultrasound of the involved extremity is the test that has replaced contrast venography for deep vein thrombosis (DVT). A D-dimer could not confirm the clot, because it is sensitive but not specific. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has had untreated varicose veins for many years. He now has persistent lower leg swelling, brown skin discoloration and a shallow ulcer above the medial ankle. Untreated long-standing varicose veins can progress to which condition?","choices":["Lymphedema","Arteriovenous fistula","Chronic venous insufficiency","Peripheral artery disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Long-standing varicose veins can progress to chronic venous insufficiency. His persistent swelling, brown skin discoloration and shallow ulcer above the medial ankle after years of untreated varicose veins fit that progression. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman who is 34 weeks pregnant sits in a car for nine hours and develops left calf swelling and pain. Her pregnancy supplies a hypercoagulable state and the long ride supplies venous stasis. Which additional component would complete the Virchow triad?","choices":["Endothelial trauma","Venous stasis","Hypercoagulable state","Arterial thrombosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Virchow triad is venous stasis, a hypercoagulable state and vascular endothelial injury. Her pregnancy supplies the hypercoagulable state and nine hours in a car supply stasis, so the missing third is endothelial trauma, as from trauma, surgery, intravenous drug use or burns. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man receiving chemotherapy for lung cancer has had swelling of his whole left leg for four days, with pitting edema in that leg and a calf 4 cm larger than the right. An ultrasound of the thigh veins shows no clot. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat the thigh scan in a week","Stop the work-up; clot excluded","Start anticoagulation for life","Ankle-brachial index"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Active cancer 1 + entire leg swollen 1 + calf swelling over 3 cm 1 + pitting edema in the symptomatic leg 1 = 4, a high likelihood. In the high-likelihood pathway a negative thigh ultrasound is followed by a lower leg ultrasound, phlebography or a repeat thigh ultrasound in 1 week. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 55-56, 61 (Wells table and flowchart pictures)"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman who smokes and takes an oral contraceptive has mild calf aching. Her Wells score is low, and her D-dimer returns positive. What is the next step?","choices":["Venous duplex ultrasound","Empiric anticoagulation alone","Reassurance and discharge","Repeat D-dimer tomorrow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her Wells score is low, so she started with a D-dimer, and a positive result leads on to ultrasound. D-dimer is sensitive but not specific, so it cannot confirm a clot, and venous duplex ultrasound has replaced contrast venography for diagnosis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 56, 57 (slide 56 flowchart picture)"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man has had symmetric pitting swelling of both ankles for three weeks. He started amlodipine for hypertension one month ago. There is no warmth, redness, tenderness or skin discoloration, and he has no history of heart, kidney or liver disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic venous insufficiency","Lymphedema","Drug-induced edema","Bilateral deep vein thrombosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symmetric pitting swelling of both ankles that began after starting amlodipine, with no warmth, redness or tenderness, fits drug-induced edema. Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as amlodipine commonly cause it; the swelling is often bilateral and usually shows no signs of inflammation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis felt a sudden sharp pain behind the right knee while climbing stairs, followed by severe calf discomfort and swelling. Venous duplex ultrasound shows no thrombus. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Superficial thrombophlebitis","Ruptured popliteal (Baker) cyst","Calf muscle tear","Post-thrombotic syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sudden pain behind the knee followed by severe, sudden calf discomfort and swelling, with a duplex ultrasound that shows no thrombus, fits a ruptured popliteal (Baker) cyst. It sits on the differential of deep vein thrombosis (DVT) because it mimics the swollen, painful calf of a clot. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man taking apixaban for deep vein thrombosis develops an acute intracranial hemorrhage. Which agent reverses the anticoagulant?","choices":["Andexanet alfa","Protamine sulfate","Idarucizumab","Vitamin K by mouth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He is taking apixaban and has an acute intracranial hemorrhage, one of the situations that calls for reversal. Andexanet alfa (Andexxa) is the reversal agent for apixaban and rivaroxaban, and it is used off-label for edoxaban. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 68"}]};
